{"step_index":191,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-30T12:07:28Z","thinking":"The user points out a potential case sensitivity issue with the word \"statement\". This suggests a confusion regarding a data type; specifically, the user may be incorrectly assuming that because the word \"statement\" is sometimes capitalized, it represents a defined data type that has case sensitivity.\n\n\nRecognizing the case difference is excellent; C++ distinguishes between `Statement` (a data type/blueprint) and `statement` (a variable instance of that type). The capitalization signifies a critical difference between a class/struct definition and an actual object. Functions like `prepare_statement` use the Type definition to declare the expected kind of variable.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"# C++ & Database Study Notes (Cheat Sheet)\\n\\nThese notes explain every new term and concept we encounter as we build our database.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. C++ Keywords & Concepts\\n\\n### Type vs. Variable Name (Capital vs. Lowercase)\\nC++ is strictly **case-sensitive** (`Statement` and `statement` are two different things!).\\n- **Capital `Statement` (The Type / Blueprint):**\\n  Defines what the data looks like. Just like `int` or `string`.\\n  ```cpp\\n  struct Statement {\\n      StatementType type;\\n  };\\n  ```\\n- **Lowercase `statement` (The Actual Variable / Object):**\\n  The actual instance created from that blueprint.\\n  ```cpp\\n  Statement statement;\\n  //  ^         ^\\n  // Type    Variable\\n  ```\\n- **Analogy:**\\n  - `Car` (Blueprint / Design) vs `my_car` (The actual car parked in your garage).\\n  - When passing into a function: you pass the **actual car** (`statement`), NOT the blueprint (`Statement`).\\n  - If you pass `Statement` with a capital S, C++ gives an error: *\\\"Statement does not refer to a value\\\"*.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Why C++ instead of C?\\n- **History:** C++ was created by Bjarne Stroustrup specifically to solve the headaches of C.\\n- **In C:** There is no `string` type. You have to manually allocate memory with `malloc()`, free it with `free()`, manage raw pointers (`char*`), and compare strings with `strcmp()`. If you make one mistake, the program crashes with a `Segmentation fault`.\\n- **In C++:** The `string` type does all of that memory allocation, resizing, and cleanup automatically under the hood. The resulting program runs just as fast, does the exact same thing, but requires 70% less boilerplate code.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### `enum` (Enumeration)\\n- **What it does:** A list of named options/choices.\\n- **Why we use it:** Instead of using numbers like `0` or `1` for commands, we give them human names:\\n  ```cpp\\n  enum StatementType {\\n      STATEMENT_INSERT,\\n      STATEMENT_SELECT\\n  };\\n  ```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### `continue;`\\n- **What it does:** Skips the rest of the current loop round and immediately jumps back \n<truncated 3615 bytes>","Description":"\"Add Type vs Variable Name (Capital vs Lowercase) to NOTES.md\"","Overwrite":"true","TargetFile":"\"/home/ashutosh-goyal/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/from-scratch-todo-course/NOTES.md\"","toolAction":"\"Updating study notes with Type vs Variable explanation\"","toolSummary":"\"Update NOTES.md with Capital vs Lowercase\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
